{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>His two sons with him, et, etc.</b> The reason he did not suffice by saying ״שְׁנֵי בָנָיו״ — and I know that they are Menasheh and Efrayim — or by saying ״מְנַשֶּׁה וְאֶפְרַיִם״ — and I know that they are the sons registered in the portion of Mikeitz — also he interrupted between the mention of the sons and the identification of their names with the word ״עִמּוֹ״, also he said ״et״ two times." "Perhaps it is because Scripture informs us of the reason for Yoseif's taking them: the taking was so that each one would receive his own blessing in its own right, apart from what would reach them through the blessing of their father. And that is his saying <b>et his two sons with him</b> — this relates to the portion that would pertain to them in his blessing; also et Menasheh, that he would be blessed in his own right; and et Efrayim, etc." "<b>Or</b> it may intend that they are worthy of blessing from two sides: on the one hand, because they are his sons; and on the other hand, because each one of them is worthy of blessing in his own right. Therefore he said <b>his sons with him</b> — also on account of their being Menasheh and Efrayim, righteous men. And he said ״עִמּוֹ״ to make a break, so that you should not understand that he is specifying who the sons are; and he said ״et״ and ״et״ to say that each one is significant in his own right:"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:52.663286793Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}